Excellent evidence: e-rara's router redirects `/<any-corpus>/content/zoom/<titleId>` to the *correct* corpus for that title (both directions verified: `zut→zuz` for the ZB title, `zuz→zut` for the ETH title, both 301 to the right place and 200 at the target).

So a viewer link of the form `https://www.e-rara.ch/zut/content/zoom/<titleId>` (or any corpus) resolves correctly. That means I can safely construct the library viewer URL from the manifest title id (which I have) — or from the record id! `record.id` = `e-rara:6507640` and the manifest is `…/i3f/v20/6507640/manifest`. So `eRaraViewerUrl(record)` can use:
1. `record.viewerUrl` if present (harvested);
2. corpus from a canonical recordUrl if present;
3. else the title id parsed from `record.iiifManifest` (or `record.id`), with corpus `zut` (any corpus redirects — verified 2026-10-18).

Let me update `eRaraViewerUrl` + tests. This gives the "Im Bibliotheks-Viewer öffnen" link for all 28 e-rara records. That's a real product improvement ("follow through to its collection").

Implementation:
```js
export function eRaraViewerUrl(record) {
  const explicit = record?.viewerUrl;
  if (typeof explicit === 'string' && /^https:\/\/www\.e-rara\.ch\/[a-z]+\/content\/zoom\/\d+/.test(explicit)) return explicit;
  const canonical = typeof record?.recordUrl === 'string' ? record.recordUrl.match(/^https:\/\/www\.e-rara\.ch\/([a-z]+)\/content\/(?:titleinfo|zoom|pageview)\/(\d+)/) : null;
  if (canonical) return `https://www.e-rara.ch/${canonical[1]}/content/zoom/${canonical[2]}`;
  const titleId = eRaraTitleId(record?.iiifManifest) ?? (typeof record?.id === 'string' ? record.id.match(/^e-rara:(\d+)$/)?.[1] : null);
  if (!titleId) return null;
  // e-rara resolves any corpus prefix to the holding library's own corpus (verified
  // 2026-10-18: /zut/…/22142031 → 301 → /zuz/…); 'zut' is only the entry point.
  return `https://www.e-rara.ch/zut/content/zoom/${titleId}`;
}
```
